WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.220
A supplier agrees to sell electricity to a factory at a fixed price for the whole of next year.

2
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:10.699
But it must buy that electricity on a market where prices move every day.

3
00:00:10.729 --> 00:00:13.215
If prices rise, the supplier loses money.

4
00:00:13.687 --> 00:00:16.238
In this episode, we explore forwards, futures,

5
00:00:16.268 --> 00:00:18.594
and how they are used to hedge price risk.

6
00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:27.178
A forward contract is a private agreement to buy or sell electricity at a fixed price for a future delivery period.

7
00:00:27.208 --> 00:00:31.990
A futures contract is similar, but it is standardised and traded on an exchange,

8
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.938
where a clearing house stands behind every trade.

9
00:00:35.823 --> 00:00:40.783
Futures are settled every day against the market price, and both sides post margin.

10
00:00:40.813 --> 00:00:43.428
That daily settlement keeps credit risk low.

11
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.290
Power futures cover standard delivery periods, such as a month, a quarter or a year,

12
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.961
and standard load profiles.

13
00:00:51.991 --> 00:00:55.056
Baseload means constant delivery around the clock.

14
00:00:55.086 --> 00:01:00.998
One megawatt of baseload for a normal year is eight thousand seven hundred sixty megawatt-hours.

15
00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:03.955
Back to our supplier.

16
00:01:03.985 --> 00:01:09.442
It sells ten megawatts of baseload to the factory at eighty-five euros per megawatt-hour.

17
00:01:09.472 --> 00:01:12.957
That is eighty-seven thousand six hundred megawatt-hours.

18
00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:18.136
To lock in its costs, it buys the same volume in the futures market at eighty euros.

19
00:01:18.623 --> 00:01:19.113
Case one:

20
00:01:19.143 --> 00:01:21.882
the average spot price rises to one hundred ten.

21
00:01:21.912 --> 00:01:22.805
Without a hedge,

22
00:01:22.835 --> 00:01:26.844
the supplier would lose two million one hundred ninety thousand euros.

23
00:01:26.874 --> 00:01:31.402
With the hedge, the futures gain two million six hundred twenty-eight thousand,

24
00:01:31.432 --> 00:01:32.960
offsetting the higher cost,

25
00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:36.364
and the margin stays at four hundred thirty-eight thousand.

26
00:01:36.857 --> 00:01:37.391
Case two:

27
00:01:37.421 --> 00:01:38.647
spot falls to sixty.

28
00:01:38.677 --> 00:01:43.165
Now the futures lose one million seven hundred fifty-two thousand euros,

29
00:01:43.195 --> 00:01:45.801
but the cheaper spot purchases compensate,

30
00:01:45.831 --> 00:01:49.504
and again the margin is four hundred thirty-eight thousand.

31
00:01:49.534 --> 00:01:52.516
The hedge removes the risk, and also the upside.

32
00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.976
Futures positions need margin.

33
00:01:56.006 --> 00:02:00.031
If the price moves against you, you pay variation margin every day,

34
00:02:00.061 --> 00:02:03.602
even though the gain on your physical position comes later.

35
00:02:03.632 --> 00:02:11.047
A two euro move on ten megawatts of annual baseload is one hundred seventy-five thousand two hundred euros in a single day.

36
00:02:11.077 --> 00:02:13.287
So hedgers must plan their liquidity.

37
00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:15.601
In Europe,

38
00:02:15.631 --> 00:02:20.341
the German Phelix baseload futures on the EEX are a widely used benchmark.

39
00:02:20.371 --> 00:02:23.543
In Southeast Europe, regional contracts exist too,

40
00:02:23.573 --> 00:02:27.002
for example Hungarian power futures listed on the EEX,

41
00:02:27.032 --> 00:02:29.757
which offer physical delivery through HUPX.

42
00:02:29.787 --> 00:02:35.073
For Serbia, check which products and rules apply with the exchanges and regulators.

43
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:37.767
Three takeaways.

44
00:02:37.797 --> 00:02:38.026
One:

45
00:02:38.056 --> 00:02:41.850
futures and forwards fix a price today for future delivery.

46
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:42.109
Two:

47
00:02:42.139 --> 00:02:46.581
a hedge locks in a margin, and gives up both the risk and the upside.

48
00:02:46.611 --> 00:02:46.970
Three:

49
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.118
futures need daily margin, so hedgers must plan their liquidity.

50
00:02:51.593 --> 00:02:54.772
Next, we look at cross-border trading and market coupling.

51
00:02:54.802 --> 00:02:56.653
Thanks for watching Power Academy.
